1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:04,179
On 28 September, the team dropped Alpha Systems.

2
00:00:04,199 --> 00:00:07,749
Two weeks later, someone says: let's stick with Alpha.

3
00:00:07,769 --> 00:00:08,869
Nobody checks.

4
00:00:08,889 --> 00:00:11,919
And the room's memory quietly rewrites itself.

5
00:00:13,245 --> 00:00:15,638
AI notetakers capture what was said.

6
00:00:15,658 --> 00:00:17,685
Memoria keeps what was decided.

7
00:00:17,705 --> 00:00:21,123
A decision has a value, a source, and a history.

8
00:00:22,401 --> 00:00:25,310
Memoria. Version control for decisions.

9
00:00:25,986 --> 00:00:29,339
A local model proposes each change with its source quote,

10
00:00:29,359 --> 00:00:32,325
and code copies the quote from the transcript.

11
00:00:32,345 --> 00:00:35,281
A person approves first values and changes.

12
00:00:35,301 --> 00:00:38,350
The record keeps earlier values with their sources.

13
00:00:38,370 --> 00:00:40,899
And if a recap repeats a replaced decision,

14
00:00:40,919 --> 00:00:43,874
Memoria can ask: “Is this a change?”

15
00:00:45,215 --> 00:00:48,657
This is the real interface, with a fictional project.

16
00:00:48,677 --> 00:00:51,358
A suggested change waits with its quote.

17
00:00:51,378 --> 00:00:53,970
Accept it, and Beta Cloud becomes current,

18
00:00:53,990 --> 00:00:57,095
while Alpha stays in history with its own words.

19
00:00:58,605 --> 00:01:00,707
It starts with speech.

20
00:01:00,727 --> 00:01:03,583
Local models turn the meeting into a transcript

21
00:01:03,603 --> 00:01:05,219
and group the voices.

22
00:01:05,239 --> 00:01:08,838
A person checks the words and names the speakers.

23
00:01:08,858 --> 00:01:12,379
Then a local, 4-billion-parameter model fills in a form,

24
00:01:12,399 --> 00:01:14,259
one token at a time.

25
00:01:14,279 --> 00:01:15,926
This reply is real.

26
00:01:15,946 --> 00:01:19,576
The quote is right: 250,000 ringgit.

27
00:01:19,596 --> 00:01:21,976
But the value has an extra zero.

28
00:01:21,996 --> 00:01:24,576
And the model wrote confidence 0.9

29
00:01:24,596 --> 00:01:27,184
for both the right values and the wrong one.

30
00:01:27,204 --> 00:01:29,792
So code checks the number against the quote,

31
00:01:29,812 --> 00:01:32,263
and the change still waits for a person.

32
00:01:33,046 --> 00:01:35,488
Every approved value keeps two clocks:

33
00:01:35,508 --> 00:01:38,257
when it applied, and when it was recorded.

34
00:01:38,277 --> 00:01:39,157
So you can ask

35
00:01:39,177 --> 00:01:42,695
what the latest record says applied on 5 October,

36
00:01:42,715 --> 00:01:46,682
or what records available by 4 November said about it.

37
00:01:49,237 --> 00:01:51,042
Ask who proposed the change,

38
00:01:51,062 --> 00:01:54,105
and the answer links to its meeting and the second.

39
00:01:54,125 --> 00:01:55,817
In this text-fed example,

40
00:01:55,837 --> 00:01:58,667
the meeting window flags a replaced vendor.

41
00:02:00,145 --> 00:02:03,850
Hosted DeepSeek, on manual transcripts of public meetings:

42
00:02:03,870 --> 00:02:06,262
the unreviewed record scored 82;

43
00:02:06,282 --> 00:02:09,400
full transcripts 81, out of 96.

44
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:11,100
No difference detected.

45
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:13,825
Mean query input was 818

46
00:02:13,845 --> 00:02:19,725
versus 31,337 tokens, excluding extraction.

47
00:02:20,159 --> 00:02:23,065
On fictional histories of up to 53 meetings,

48
00:02:23,085 --> 00:02:26,741
transcript search fell from 22 to 13 correct.

49
00:02:26,761 --> 00:02:30,110
The record went from 28 to 24.

50
00:02:30,130 --> 00:02:33,306
In one measured live-path replay of about two minutes,

51
00:02:33,326 --> 00:02:37,469
Memoria's 14 processes sent 0 external bytes.

52
00:02:37,996 --> 00:02:40,467
On a partial set of fictional text probes,

53
00:02:40,487 --> 00:02:43,629
useful warnings rose from 3 to 10 of 12,

54
00:02:43,649 --> 00:02:46,504
with 0 false warnings in both builds.

55
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:49,728
Decision extraction is the weak step.

56
00:02:49,748 --> 00:02:52,766
Unreviewed, it scored 59 of 96.

57
00:02:52,786 --> 00:02:54,992
An empty record scored 58.

58
00:02:55,012 --> 00:02:57,792
On the 38 questions that need a recorded value

59
00:02:57,812 --> 00:02:59,667
or a nonzero change count,

60
00:02:59,687 --> 00:03:03,104
it got 18, against 26 for full transcripts

61
00:03:03,124 --> 00:03:04,904
and 23 for search.

62
00:03:04,924 --> 00:03:07,116
Proposed changes wait for review,

63
00:03:07,136 --> 00:03:10,492
but review accuracy and effort are still unmeasured.

64
00:03:11,444 --> 00:03:15,074
Next: decision audits for boards and investment committees.

65
00:03:15,094 --> 00:03:17,024
Governance without re-listening.

66
00:03:17,044 --> 00:03:19,924
And a memory that survives staff turnover.

67
00:03:20,514 --> 00:03:21,240
Memoria.

68
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:23,127
Keep decisions checkable.
